meta {
  name: 60 TAT Flow E2E
  seq: 66
}

script:pre-request {
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // TAT Flow E2E — folder-level setup (runs before every request in this folder).
  // Seeds baseUrl + the ONLY values a developer edits (once): the entry
  // business-admin credentials + tenant slug. Everything else (tokens, ids) is
  // captured automatically by each request's post-response via bru.setVar(...).
  //
  // Just click "Next" through 01 → 28 (or Run Folder). No manual input required.
  // A per-run unique id (tatRunId) is generated so unique fields (branch name,
  // usernames, emails, mobiles, test/panel codes) don't collide on re-runs.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  if (!bru.getVar("baseUrl")) bru.setVar("baseUrl", "http://localhost:3000/api/v1");

  // Entry point — the SEEDED platform SiteAdmin (prisma/seed.ts). This is the ONLY
  // credential the flow assumes exists; the tenant + business admin are created
  // at runtime (steps 01-02), so nothing else needs editing. Change these two
  // only if your seeded SiteAdmin differs.
  if (!bru.getVar("siteAdminEmail"))    bru.setVar("siteAdminEmail", "admin@kalnostics.com");
  if (!bru.getVar("siteAdminPassword")) bru.setVar("siteAdminPassword", "SuperSecret1");

  // Known password for the operator user this flow creates (so login is automated).
  if (!bru.getVar("operatorPassword")) bru.setVar("operatorPassword", "Str0ng@Pass1");

  // One-time per-run unique suffix + derived unique mobiles (10-digit, distinct
  // by prefix). Guarded so it is computed once and reused for the whole run.
  // (adminIdentifier / adminPassword / tenantSlug are NOT set here — they are
  // captured from the tenant created in step 02.)
  if (!bru.getVar("tatRunId")) {
    const n = String(Date.now());
    bru.setVar("tatRunId", n.slice(-6));
    bru.setVar("adminBootstrapPhone", "+9195" + n.slice(-8));
    bru.setVar("operatorMobile", "98" + n.slice(-8));
    bru.setVar("phleboMobile",   "97" + n.slice(-8));
    bru.setVar("patientMobile",  "96" + n.slice(-8));
  }
}

docs {
  # TAT Flow E2E — fully automated, click-Next end-to-end

  Proves the **Turnaround Time (TAT)** feature end-to-end: from a Business-Admin
  login, through branch/user/catalogue setup, an order, the accession
  **Accept** (TAT starts) and the technician **Approve** (TAT stops), to reading
  the computed TAT back off the Technician Report listing + breakdown.

  ## How to run
  Select this folder and **Run Folder**, or click **Next** through requests
  01 → 30 in order. Each request captures the ids/tokens the next one needs via
  `bru.setVar(...)` — nothing is copied by hand. A `tatRunId` is generated once
  per run so re-runs never collide on unique fields.

  ## The only thing you may need to edit
  Nothing, on a normal dev DB. The flow bootstraps itself from the **seeded
  SiteAdmin** (`admin@kalnostics.com` / `SuperSecret1`) and creates its own
  tenant + business admin at runtime. Edit `siteAdminEmail` / `siteAdminPassword`
  in this pre-request only if your seeded SiteAdmin differs.

  ## Flow
  **Bootstrap:** 01 SiteAdmin login → 02 create tenant + business admin (captures
  the new admin's phone + one-time temp password).

  **Setup:** 03 business-admin login → 04 create branch (registration + accession
  + lab_operations + phlebotomist enabled) → 05 create the branch **operating
  schedule** (required so TAT can exclude non-working time) → 06 create operator
  user → 07 enable operator modules → 08 grant permissions → 09 operator login
  (branch-scoped token).

  **Catalogue + order:** 10 phlebotomist → 11 phlebotomist schedule → 12 patient
  → 13 resolve branch master data → 14 lab test (with **Max TAT** configured) →
  15 lab panel → 16/17 import into branch → 18/19 resolve branch ids → 20 create
  the (in-center) diagnostic order → generates an accession sample.

  **TAT validation:** 21 list samples → 22 collect → **23 Accept (TAT START)** →
  24 resolve technician report → 25 submit → 26 validate → **27 Approve (TAT
  STOP)** → **28 Technician Report Listing** (shows `tat` per row) → **29 Get TAT
  breakdown** (verify status / time / breach) → 30 TAT analytics summary.

  ## What the final requests let you verify
  - **26 Technician Report Listing** — each row carries
    `tat: { band, netMinutes, isFinal, computable }`.
  - **27 Get TAT breakdown** — `{ band, netMinutes, maxTatMinutes, isFinal,
    startAt, endAt, reason }`. `band === "BREACHED"` means the TAT was breached;
    `netMinutes` is the working TAT time; `band` is the TAT status.
  - Because accept→approve happen seconds apart during the run and the operating
    schedule is open all day, the band will be **WITHIN** with `netMinutes ≈ 0`
    (not breached) — the happy path. To force other bands, lower the test's
    Max TAT (request 12) or space the accept/approve apart.
}
